    "statement": h1_statement,
    "grounded_in": ["OBSERVATION 2", "OBSERVATION 3"],
    "mechanism": (
        "OBSERVATION 2 and 3 show very strong reciprocal enrichment of PX and PY as each other’s spatial neighbors, "
        "indicating repeated, tight PX–PY communities rather than random mixing. Such recurrent spatial interfaces "
        "often reflect shared signaling or stress-response programs that differentiate interface cells from the "
        "same populations away from these contacts."
    ),
    "test_plan": (
        "1) Define PX–PY interface cells as PX or PY cells that have at least one nearest neighbor of the partner "
        "label within the 14-neighbor graph; define non-interface PX and PY cells as those with zero partner-label "
        "neighbors. 2) Within PX and within PY separately, perform differential expression comparing interface vs "
        "non-interface cells using a rank-based test (e.g., Wilcoxon) across all 238 genes, controlling for sample "
        "identity. 3) Identify gene sets of at least five genes that are consistently upregulated in both PX and PY "
        "interface cells relative to their non-interface counterparts. 4) Optionally summarize these shared "
        "upregulated genes by principal component analysis to show a common program. The hypothesis is supported "
        "if a multi-gene module is significantly and coherently upregulated in interface PX and PY, and not present "
        "in non-interface cells."
    ),
    "predicted_outcome": (
        "If the hypothesis holds, both PX and PY interface subsets will show a shared pattern where multiple (≥5) "
